1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:11,360
While safety improvements might have been made to our streets in recent years, transport studies also show declines in pedestrian mobility, especially among young children.
虽然近年来我们的街道在安全方面可能有所改善，但交通研究也显示，行人的出行能力在下降，在年幼的孩子中尤为明显。

2
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:19,500
Many parents say there’s too much traffic on the roads for their children to walk safely to school, so they pack them into the car instead.
许多家长说，路上车太多，孩子没法安全地步行上学，所以他们改为把孩子塞进车里，开车送去上学。

3
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:25,790
Dutch authors Thalia Verkade and Marco te Brömmelstroet are bothered by facts like these.
荷兰作者塔利娅·费尔卡德和马尔科·特·布罗梅尔斯特鲁特对这样的事实感到困扰。

4
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:34,992
In their new book Movement: How to Take Back Our Streets and Transform Our Lives, they call for a rethink of our streets and the role they play in our lives.
在他们的新书《出行：如何夺回我们的街道，改变我们的生活》中，他们呼吁重新思考我们的街道，以及街道在我们生活中所扮演的角色。

5
00:00:35,792 --> 00:00:38,832
Life on city streets started to change decades ago.
城市街道上的生活早在几十年前就开始改变了。

6
00:00:39,632 --> 00:00:44,952
Whole neighbourhoods were destroyed to make way for new road networks and kids had to play elsewhere.
整片整片的街区被拆毁，为新的道路网络让路，孩子们只好到别处去玩。

7
00:00:45,752 --> 00:00:47,332
Some communities fought back.
一些社区奋起反抗。

8
00:00:48,132 --> 00:00:56,602
Most famously, a Canadian journalist who lived in Manhattan in the 1950s led a campaign to stop the destruction of her local park.
最有名的是，一位20世纪50年代住在曼哈顿的加拿大记者领导了一场运动，阻止当地公园被毁。

9
00:00:57,402 --> 00:01:07,902
Describing her alarm at its proposed replacement with an expressway, Jane Jacobs called on her mayor to champion “New York as a decent place to live, and not just rush through.”
简·雅各布斯表达了她对有人提议用高速公路取代这座公园的担忧，呼吁市长捍卫“纽约作为一个宜居之地，而不只是一个匆匆路过的地方”。

10
00:01:08,702 --> 00:01:14,572
Similar campaigns occurred in Australia in the late 1960s and 1970s as well.
20世纪60年代末和70年代，澳大利亚也发生过类似的运动。

11
00:01:15,372 --> 00:01:23,662
Although these campaigns were widespread, the reality is that the majority of western cities were completely redesigned around the needs of the motor car.
尽管这些运动很普遍，但现实是，大多数西方城市都完全围绕汽车的需要重新设计了。

12
00:01:24,462 --> 00:01:27,522
The number of cars on roads has been increasing rapidly.
路上的汽车数量一直在迅速增加。

13
00:01:28,322 --> 00:01:36,512
In Australia we now have over twenty million cars for just over twenty-six million people, among the highest rates of car ownership in the world.
在澳大利亚，如今我们2600万出头的人口拥有2000多万辆汽车，汽车拥有率位居世界前列。

14
00:01:37,312 --> 00:01:42,362
We invest a lot in roads that help us rush through, but we fail to account for the true costs.
我们在能让我们匆匆通过的道路上投入很多，却没有把真正的代价考虑在内。

15
00:01:43,162 --> 00:01:49,562
Do we really recognise what it costs us as a society when children can’t move safely around our communities?
当孩子们无法在社区里安全走动时，我们真的意识到这让整个社会付出了什么代价吗？

16
00:01:50,362 --> 00:01:56,124
The authors of Movement have it right: it’s time to think differently about that street outside your front door.
《出行》的作者说得对：是时候换个角度看待你家门外的那条街了。
